"Transforming graph states using single-qubit operations (https://arxiv.org/html/1805.05305v2)\nciteturn9555view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/1805.05305v2\",\"pattern\":\"Definition 2.1\"}); Total lines: 821\nL178: ### 2.3 Local Clifford operations\nL179: \nL180: Let’s consider the following sequence of single-qubit Clifford operations\nL181: \nL182:  | $$U_{v}^{(G)}=\\exp\\left(-\\mathrm{i}\\frac{\\pi}{4}X_{v}\\right)\\prod_{u\\in N_{v}}\\exp\\left(\\mathrm{i}\\frac{\\pi}{4}Z_{u}\\right).$$  |  | (15)\nL183: \nL184: As shown in [cite51†20 ], the operation $U_{v}^{(G)}$ on the state $|G\\rangle$ can be seen as an operation on the graph $G$ since\nL185: \nL186:  | $$U_{v}^{(G)}|G\\rangle=|\\tau_{v}(G)\\rangle$$  |  | (16)\nL187: where $\\tau_{v}$ is a local complementation on the vertex $v$, as defined in definition cite53†2.1 and illustrated in equation (cite54†18 ).\nL188: ###### Definition 2.1 (local complementation).\nL189: \nL190: A local complementation $\\tau_{v}$ acts on a vertex $v$ of a graph $G$ by complementing the induced subgraph on the neighborhood of $v$. The neighborhoods of the graph $\\tau_{v}(G)$ are therefore given by\nL191: \nL192:  | $$N_{u}^{(\\tau_{v}(G))}=\\begin{cases}N_{u}\\Delta(N_{v}\\setminus\\{u\\})&\\quad\\text{if }(u,v)\\in E(G)\\\\\nL193: N_{u}&\\quad\\text{else}\\end{cases},$$  |  | (17)\nL194: \nL195: where $\\Delta$ denotes the symmetric difference between two sets.\nL196:  | $$\\raisebox{-27.60063pt}{\\includegraphics[scale={0.5}]{tadpole1.pdf}}\\quad\\xrightarrow{\\tau_{2}}\\quad\\raisebox{-27.60063pt}{\\includegraphics[scale={0.5}]{tadpole2.pdf}}$$  |  | (18)\nL197: \nL198: Surprisingly, any single-qubit Clifford operation which takes some graph state to another graph state can seen as some sequence of local complementations on the corresponding graphs. This was proven in [cite37†32 ] and we restate this theorem here.\nL199: ###### Theorem 2.1 (Van den Nest [cite37†32 ]).\nL200: \nL201: Two graph states $|G\\rangle$ and $|G^{\\prime}\\rangle$ are equivalent under single-qubit Clifford operations if and only if their corresponding graphs $G$ and $G^{\\prime}$ are related by some sequence of local complementations.\nL202: \nL203: Let $m=v_{1}v_{2}\\dots v_{l}$ be a sequence of vertices of $G$, then we denote the sequence of local complementations on the vertices in $m$ as\nL204: \nL205:  | $$\\tau_{m}(G)=\\tau_{v_{l}}\\circ\\dots\\circ\\tau_{v_{2}}\\circ\\tau_{v_{1}}(G).$$  |  | (19)\nL206: If there exists a sequence $m$ such that $\\tau_{m}(G)=G^{\\prime}$ then we write this as $G\\sim_{\\mathrm{LC}}G^{\\prime}$. Theorem cite55†2.1 can therefore be stated as\nL207: \nL208:  | $$|G\\rangle\\sim_{\\mathrm{LC}}|G^{\\prime}\\rangle\\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad G\\sim_{\\mathrm{LC}}G^{\\prime}.$$  |  | (20)\nL209: \nL210: Testing whether two graphs are LC-equivalent can be done in time $\\mathcal{O}(n^{4})$, where $n$ is the size of the graphs, as shown in [cite39†8 ].\nL211: ### 2.4 Local Pauli measurements\nL212: \nL213: How should the corresponding graph of a graph state be updated when a measurement is performed? In [cite56†21 ] it is shown how the Pauli projectors $P_{v}^{(X,\\pm)}$, $P_{v}^{(Y,\\pm)}$, $P_{v}^{(Z,\\pm)}$ act on graph states^{3}^{3} 3 For the special case when $|N_{v}|=0$, a measurement in the $X$-basis does not change the graph state since this is then $|G\\rangle=|+\\rangle_{v}\\otimes|G\\setminus v\\rangle$.\nL214:  | $\\displaystyle P_{v}^{(Z,\\pm)}|G\\rangle$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{2}|Z,\\pm\\rangle_{v}\\otimes U_{v}^{(Z,\\pm)}|G\\setminus v\\rangle$  |  | (21)\nL215:  | $\\displaystyle P_{v}^{(Y,\\pm)}|G\\rangle$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{2}|Y,\\pm\\rangle_{v}\\otimes U_{v}^{(Y,\\pm)}|\\tau_{v}(G)\\setminus v\\rangle$  |  | (22)\nL216:  | $\\displaystyle P_{v}^{(X,\\pm)}|G\\rangle$  | $\\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{2}|X,\\pm\\rangle_{v}\\otimes U_{e}^{(X,\\pm)}|T_{e}(G)\\setminus v\\rangle\\quad\\text{ if }|N_{v}|>0$  |  | (23)\nL217: where $e$ is an edge of $G$ incident on the vertex $v$. Choosing a different edge $e^{\\prime}$ incident on $v$ gives a single-qubit Clifford-equivalent graph state, i.e. $|T_{e^{\\prime}}(G)\\setminus v\\rangle\\sim_{\\mathrm{LC}}|T_{e}(G)\\setminus v\\rangle$. The operation $T_{(u,v)}$ is called a pivot and is defined as $T_{(u,v)}=\\tau_{v}\\circ\\tau_{u}\\circ\\tau_{v}$. The pivot can simply be specified by an undirected edge since\nL218:  | $$\\tau_{v}\\circ\\tau_{u}\\circ\\tau_{v}(G)=\\tau_{u}\\circ\\tau_{v}\\circ\\tau_{u}(G)\\quad\\text{if }(u,v)\\in E(G)$$  |  | (24)\nL219: \nL221:  | $$U_{v}^{(Z,+)}=\\mathbb{I}_{v},\\quad U_{v}^{(Z,-)}=\\prod_{u\\in N_{v}}Z_{u}.$$  |  | (25)\nL222: \nL223: Since the correction operators are sequences of single-qubit Clifford operations it is therefore the case that\nL224: \nL225:  | $$|G\\setminus v\\rangle,\\quad|\\tau_{v}(G)\\setminus v\\rangle,\\quad|T_{e}(G)\\setminus v\\rangle$$  |  | (26)\nL226: \nL227: are all qubit-minors of $|G\\rangle$. Furthermore, Bouchet proved the following.\nL228: ###### Lemma 2.1 (Bouchet, (9.2) in [cite57†7 ]).\nL229: \nL230: If $G\\sim_{\\mathrm{LC}}G^{\\prime}$ then $G^{\\prime}\\setminus v$ is LC-equivalent to $G\\setminus v$, $\\tau_{v}(G)\\setminus v$ or $T_{e}(G)\\setminus v$, where $e$ is some fixed edge incident on $v$ in $G$.\nL231: \nL232: We therefore have the following lemma.\nL233: ###### Lemma 2.2.\nL234: \nL235: Let $|G\\rangle$ be a graph state, $v\\in V(G)$ be a vertex and $e\\in E(G)$ be an edge incident on $v$. Furthermore, assume that $|G^{\\prime}\\rangle$ is a qubit-minor of $|G\\rangle$, where $V(G^{\\prime})=V(G)\\setminus v$ and that $G^{\\prime}$ has no vertices of degree zero. Then $|G^{\\prime}\\rangle$ is single-qubit Clifford-equivalent to at least one of the three states in equation (cite58†26 ).\nL237: Since $|G^{\\prime}\\rangle$ is a qubit-minor of $|G\\rangle$ we know that there exists a sequence $\\mathcal{W}$ of single-qubit Clifford operations, single-qubit Pauli measurements and classical communication that takes $|G\\rangle$ to $|G^{\\prime}\\rangle$, by definition. Any single-qubit Pauli measurement on a qubit $u$ gives a product states between qubit $u$ and the rest of the qubits, see equations (cite59†21 )-(cite60†23 ).\nL238: The sequence $\\mathcal{W}$ cannot therefore contain a single-qubit Pauli measurement on a qubit $u$, different from $v$, since $u$ has non-zero degree in the graph $G^{\\prime}$ by assumption. Without loss of generality we can in fact assume that $\\mathcal{W}$ is a sequence of single-qubit Clifford operations followed by a measurement of $v$ in the standard basis and then by another sequence of single-qubit Clifford operations.\nL239: The reason why it is sufficient to consider a measurement in the standard basis is that any other Pauli measurement can be simulated by performing some single-qubit Clifford operation followed by a measurement in the standard basis. Assume that the sequence of single-qubit Clifford operations before (after) the measurement is described by the sequences of local complementations $m$ ($m^{\\prime}$), which exists due to theorem cite55†2.1 . We therefore have that\nL240:  | $$\\tau_{m^{\\prime}}(\\tau_{m}(G)\\setminus v)=G^{\\prime}.$$  |  | (27)\nL241: \nL242: Using lemma cite61†2.1 we have that $\\tau_{m}(G)\\setminus v$, and therefore $G^{\\prime}$, is LC-equivalent to either $G\\setminus v$, $\\tau_{v}(G)\\setminus v$ or $T_{e}(G)\\setminus v$. Finally, by theorem cite55†2.1 the lemma follows. ∎\nL243: ### 2.5 Vertex-minors\nL244: \nL245: As mentioned, the question of whether $|G^{\\prime}\\rangle$ is a qubit-minor of $|G\\rangle$ is equivalent to whether $G^{\\prime}$ is a vertex-minor of $G$. Vertex-minors were introduced by Bouchet in [cite57†7 ] but by the name of $l$-reductions.\nL246: ###### Definition 2.2 (vertex-minor).\nL247: \nL248: Let $G$ be a graph. $G^{\\prime}$ is called a vertex-minor of $G$ if it can be reached by some sequence of local complementations and vertex-deletions. Equivalently, $G^{\\prime}$ is called a vertex-minor of $G$ if there exists a sequence of vertices $m$ such that\nL249: \nL250:  | $$\\tau_{m}(G)[V(G^{\\prime})]=G^{\\prime}$$  |  | (28)\nL251: \nL252: If $G^{\\prime}$ is a vertex-minor of $G$, we denote this as\nL253: \nL254:  | $$G^{\\prime}<G$$  |  | (29)\nL255: In the previous sections we have seen that single-qubit Clifford operations that take graph states to graph states can be seen as local complementations on the corresponding graph and similarly for single-qubit Pauli measurements and vertex-deletions. The relation between qubit-minors and vertex-minors is captured by the following theorem.\nL275: ###### Definition 2.3 (cut-rank).\nL276: \nL277: Let’s assume that $A$ is a subset of the vertices $V$ of some graph $G$ with adjacency matrix $\\Gamma$. The cut-rank $\\cutrk_{A}(G)$ of $G$ with respect to $A$, is then defined as\nL278: \nL279:  | $$\\cutrk_{A}(G)\\equiv\\rank_{\\mathbb{F}_{2}}(\\Gamma[A,V\\setminus A]),$$  |  | (33)\nL280: \nL281: where $\\rank_{\\mathbb{F}_{2}}$ is the rank over the finite field of order two.\nL282: \nL283: Note that the cut-rank is symmetric in the sense that\nL284:  | $$\\cutrk_{A}(G)=\\rank_{\\mathbb{F}_{2}}(\\Gamma[A,V\\setminus A])=\\rank_{\\mathbb{F}_{2}}(\\Gamma[V\\setminus A,A]^{\\top})=\\cutrk_{V\\setminus A}(G).$$  |  | (34)\nL285: \nL286: Interestingly the cut-rank with respect to $A$ of a graph $G$ is in fact equal to the Schmidt-rank of the state $|G\\rangle$ with respect to the bipartition $(A,V\\setminus A)$.^{4}^{4} 4 This is proven in [cite51†20 ], see proposition 10.\nL287: \nL288: Next we define what is called a rank-decomposition of a graph.\nL289: ###### Definition 2.4 (rank-decomposition).\nL290: A rank-decomposition of a graph $G$ is a pair $\\mathcal{R}=(\\mathcal{T},\\mu)$, where $\\mathcal{T}$ is a subcubic tree and $\\mu$ is a bijection $\\mu:V(G)\\rightarrow\\{l:l\\text{ is a leaf of }\\mathcal{T}\\}$. A subcubic tree is a tree with at least two vertices and each vertex has degree less or equal to $3$. Any edge $e$ in $\\mathcal{T}$ splits the tree into two connected components upon deletion and therefore induces a partition $(A_{e},B_{e})$ of the leaves.\nL299: ###### Definition 2.5 (rank-width).\nL300: \nL301: The rank-width $\\rwd(G)$ of a graph $G$ is the minimum width over all rank-decompositions, i.e.\nL302: \nL303:  | $$\\rwd(G)\\equiv\\min_{\\mathcal{R}}\\width_{\\mathcal{R}}(G)=\\min_{(\\mathcal{T},\\mu)}\\max_{e\\in E(\\mathcal{T})}\\cutrk_{\\mu^{-1}(\\mathcal{T},e)}(G).$$  |  | (37)\nL392: If there exists a $P$ in $\\mathcal{P}_{U}$ such that $G^{\\prime}\\sim_{\\mathrm{LC}}P(G)$ then we clearly have that $G^{\\prime}<G$, since any such $P$ is some sequence of local complementations and vertex-deletions. Assume now that $G^{\\prime}<G$. We will prove by induction on $n-k$ that there exists a $P$ in $\\mathcal{P}_{U}$ such that $G^{\\prime}\\sim_{\\mathrm{LC}}P(G)$. For $n-k=1$ this follows directly from lemma cite61†2.1 . Assume therefore that it is true for $n-k=l$.\nL394:  | $$\\exists P\\in\\mathcal{P}_{U\\setminus\\{v\\}}\\;:\\;\\tilde{G}\\sim_{\\mathrm{LC}}P(G).$$  |  | (41)\nL395: \nL396: Then from lemma cite61†2.1 we know that $G^{\\prime}=\\tilde{G}\\setminus v$ is LC-equivalent to at least one of the following graphs\nL397: \nL398:  | $$P(G)\\setminus v,\\quad\\tau_{v}(P(G))\\setminus v,\\quad T_{v}(P(G))\\setminus v$$  |  | (42)\nL399: \nL400: and the theorem follows. ∎\nL401: \nL402: From theorem cite67†3.1 we see that to check if $G^{\\prime}$ is a vertex-minor of $G$ it is sufficient to check if one of the graphs in\nL412: Each local complementation can be done in quadratic time in the size of the graph. From the definition of local complementation, definition cite53†2.1 , we see that this can be done by adding the row of the adjacency matrix corresponding to the vertex where the local complementation is performed to the rows of its neighbors, where adding means vector-addition modulo 2. We therefore have that the running time of the full algorithm has the following recursive expression\n"
